#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110382 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "42313322-7535-5969-ad84-958449e48c1b" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3480" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\dfrac{5}{9}$. What is the $x$-coordinate of $A$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{5}{9}\n$$\n\ninto the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2+y^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] x^2+\\left(\\dfrac{5}{9}\\right)^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] x^2+\\left(\\dfrac{25}{81}\\right)&=1 \\\\[5pt] x^2&= 1-\\dfrac{25}{81} \\\\[5pt] x^2&= \\dfrac{56}{81} \\\\[5pt] x&= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{56}{81}} \\\\[5pt] x&= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive. So, we select the positive answer.\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110383 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "02c747c2-0eda-5c53-8e43-829f878dd278" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3482" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2}{3}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1453/Source/Images/a3304e0765fc9114069ac2c32af82d61.png)" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\sin^{2} θ &= 1 - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{2}{3})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{4}{9}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant.\n\nSo our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 5}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "bbf71023-510c-54af-9d0c-4df6744e3007" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3484" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\csc\\theta$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1453/Source/Images/fbbb469f7aac22ff545c74f910fb8881.png)" :question/worked-solution "Again, any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\sin^{2} θ &= 1 - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{24}{49}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{5}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{5}{7}\n$$\n\nFinally, knowing that\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110385 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "ba6cc6a0-dbb8-58d2-9d02-c76310028d4e" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3485" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{4}{5}$, what is $\\tan\\theta$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1453/Source/Images/e45c2103a055d4e2fe29c84aa4497c7a.png)" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\sin^{2} θ &= 1 - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{4}{5})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{16}{25}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe finally get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac {3}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fb437ea-e614-5770-8300-0cc9185ecf54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244720} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0e3134-1b03-57ad-8c14-d8e28988878e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244721} {:answer/id #uuid "e84aa24b-382f-515a-8cf8-bfd552d84928" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244722} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d76b8a-3f2c-56e3-9b5f-5a18a6748cc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244723} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5d66a0-f342-5332-b267-1ff4540fa776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d0e3134-1b03-57ad-8c14-d8e28988878e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244721} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbc36ff3-d93c-5137-aefe-14f40d0a6ced" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244719}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c31e5dd5-162f-5697-8eab-a49856c3ff0a" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124608" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{2}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae94c492-93d1-57dc-94d3-e68c5cb65ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244727} {:answer/id #uuid "2022a637-3e2a-51d3-b37c-a8410d972acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244728} {:answer/id #uuid "cb98b101-2e12-5ccf-9673-076950072586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244729} {:answer/id #uuid "56630b9b-e34e-5433-8a04-0e317a645558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244730} {:answer/id #uuid "8eddb895-6583-5c9d-ba30-99125760fcce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2022a637-3e2a-51d3-b37c-a8410d972acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244728} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba315678-9027-5566-a76c-2076b2357fb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244726}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c8002d8-e0b3-5c8f-88f0-b9c94fad04ff" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124611" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{5}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8d37b4e-3b31-540b-8fda-52bbc40d89d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244734} {:answer/id #uuid "a89340e4-602c-5b32-acd6-6b90c409b688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244735} {:answer/id #uuid "dd443963-319b-59ab-9096-5efd22c3775c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244736} {:answer/id #uuid "0a9d64de-50d9-5340-913f-14067c363b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244737} {:answer/id #uuid "05bb04ea-a3d5-5907-8a3b-bf0ad1cffe9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a89340e4-602c-5b32-acd6-6b90c409b688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244735} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f76ca05-7aa7-5b6e-ade1-26790e017c88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244733}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5df5974-b003-594f-82f8-0b69c911ae6c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124613" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{16}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{16} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ec8601e-121f-5a16-a1ac-a3a519551281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244741} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c23f4c-182f-5bad-95e7-a406b53f20ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244742} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3498e8-5a0b-5310-9e17-0b5cd1b84d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244743} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a8f537-0410-55c3-b01d-3ec8a378925c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244744} {:answer/id #uuid "03938b3a-cc46-5bd0-b4fc-3e78c371893b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d3498e8-5a0b-5310-9e17-0b5cd1b84d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244743} :answer-field/id #uuid "559a3139-758d-5080-9b05-e954fd554690" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244740}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab93d133-79c0-514a-b98d-36a4fd849be1" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124625" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/e50a755a20330d7f0eca8d02c8ddab33f392d5b70f7ca35b4dc514e22e61141c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{6}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{36}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c0e3508-e882-50e4-bc97-242e3a47e51b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244748} {:answer/id #uuid "173a3a92-a23d-55ac-ae5e-2ea5f6d52636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244749} {:answer/id #uuid "008e69dd-b5c9-5869-a946-4d21571bbf31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244750} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e511f7-c7c4-5b50-8c8c-557644bf0e41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244751} {:answer/id #uuid "833f6792-a8a1-51cd-9cc9-b82ee54e2be3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "008e69dd-b5c9-5869-a946-4d21571bbf31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244750} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b6f2673-fb9a-57f1-993c-5c1d4a842e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244747}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ae77c0b-34b8-5a79-a5be-337b58c15915" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124743" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/02cb693b74f0f315389f87ceed1674da11f661b3363e4df0890bdf82d61cc60c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $csc⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/a479e8e7906a3a0dc43e925c5bccd6534b4ab2e02a289403b2d0c92d0b0eb1d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncsc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e11348d0-6f04-5538-8c61-81cb1bcfce75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107212} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd673ca-a545-5697-a3e3-8fcd04384a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107213} {:answer/id #uuid "2c5ee479-bd5c-516f-9781-5c72af5726c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107214} {:answer/id #uuid "6a031f8a-1b43-5f03-a34c-cc10eefef83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107215} {:answer/id #uuid "6e223962-8898-546e-a8b6-d3577a292fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c5ee479-bd5c-516f-9781-5c72af5726c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107214} :answer-field/id #uuid "2875f717-ca5d-504c-966a-a3608a5a07bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107211}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2755d3ad-f502-5322-8d2b-3addfcafe3f4" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124750" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1453/Source/Images/q-124750.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{5}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sec θ$." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/734eef45985ddfb6450666e1dd32c6d3beab9ca3aef66c87c6ba393d6960b08b.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{1}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{2\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bf33e81-de89-5b65-a086-a16b5f8e7b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244755} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b31a96-ffc8-5e7b-b811-77efc992d770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{54}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244756} {:answer/id #uuid "217d1d2f-d7a5-54a6-a8c5-6b19825ec5ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244757} {:answer/id #uuid "44d5d213-4d0a-52ee-bc34-4cc787301607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244758} {:answer/id #uuid "93751721-87fe-5a06-b0c5-b52032b7d16a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93751721-87fe-5a06-b0c5-b52032b7d16a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244759} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa8fc0b-e54e-551d-932b-5d4b583755b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244754}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6ebeb53-e60f-5b2b-ac88-2cb095235d53" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124789" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/aad0b38f5afb88c13039ac577a927e232d84905de40297ed824c966795ce7bb4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/e2323a87ec4ab790714f4f6f5e5ff359495e93c6be5d76ae4b9f7ff15d14fd90.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{14}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{11}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{11}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14\\cdot 11}}{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b12489f9-47f5-5b99-a3f9-827300c18a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244762} {:answer/id #uuid "c32b75c8-8b4d-59cc-8c26-962e22445c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244763} {:answer/id #uuid "10e2511c-8111-59c0-bf2d-bb44bd2b3b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244764} {:answer/id #uuid "72587a39-f71b-5281-bf1b-c1e7029df0ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244765} {:answer/id #uuid "727628e1-2b2c-52db-89eb-d88d368bdee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72587a39-f71b-5281-bf1b-c1e7029df0ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244765} :answer-field/id #uuid "35b84333-5685-56f4-8cb6-f32ebe08d3d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244761}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31a24d95-250d-52f5-aac3-20c4a9ac5e80" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124791" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/c2938adc4474cec0da06b5cee00b60110b7e7be86d2b0cec3f1cff99653b8f76.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ what is $tan⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/af76b605cad900f45bc9def13e9ec2ffb12cb253ff0bdd4c07e764eac2a30937.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{12}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5})}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13\\cdot 3}}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d04bc4-3bff-50b4-b662-74a76afb9681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244769} {:answer/id #uuid "08fa91d8-8129-5e31-95d7-afe0a2a26f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244770} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8b0294-8fce-55b0-9272-b50f3acd1794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244771} {:answer/id #uuid "30287c74-7cc6-56dc-8745-5d5162362071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244772} {:answer/id #uuid "77c5261a-bec0-5a29-a6f8-4006aed9b871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08fa91d8-8129-5e31-95d7-afe0a2a26f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244770} :answer-field/id #uuid "a29aad9d-5535-5e82-b476-8eef87401732" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244768}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37088781-ccf5-559d-b0c3-0452b2220ff3" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-23216" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{1}{4}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{16}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{15}{16} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01346b90-c281-56d5-bbc9-27c0b953b25b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244776} {:answer/id #uuid "6c76de67-5175-5569-94cc-5df78980cd74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244777} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b711d9-0a61-516e-b32f-dd73ddc5f6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244778} {:answer/id #uuid "4902b10f-41a5-54c7-a5e8-cb1fc3f7d8d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244779} {:answer/id #uuid "a71b3a2f-3385-5981-a9c7-c5e8382703ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4902b10f-41a5-54c7-a5e8-cb1fc3f7d8d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244779} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c2731e8-6c66-5103-b8c7-b1f4cc1bd6c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244775}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92855669-74b1-5c98-b515-77b010a48943" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-37899" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/f4fdf6f8d819a4ee043b1cc3b86d795e3166ebfe181e0958246dc084c2f9c6fc.png)\n\nThe point $P$ lies on the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{3}{7},$ use the Pythagorean Identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{3}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{40}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e27fa36-a5ef-5848-98c0-7f72b264ed62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244783} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4d1e0d-f58c-589a-8e51-2249af6c384e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244784} {:answer/id #uuid "9fee02c0-effd-5e07-bbd4-c037e2ba2b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244785} {:answer/id #uuid "ce77f37d-58fa-57ab-b1ad-32be6f8bac8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244786} {:answer/id #uuid "52f3f149-4d2b-5179-ada7-61331cd2c5fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fee02c0-effd-5e07-bbd4-c037e2ba2b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244785} :answer-field/id #uuid "2769842c-de42-5444-b0a8-f64b39b160b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244782}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5108857e-4a0b-577d-ab56-bb8f18245f20" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-37915" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/6c36dbce847c719accc77fa15f33dfb9a87cfae669691afe30f7693663076d2e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/52fe94bb303a001e89f9db221e9730d4ca8c5c284217aa20fb6084ee041dec66.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{10}{16}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{20}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5ef0bc3-c240-524b-b40a-3503bb0b8b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244790} {:answer/id #uuid "68446250-0675-5e89-b707-0653b9d79937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244791} {:answer/id #uuid "cea943c2-5640-59a3-a907-317a28833ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244792} {:answer/id #uuid "37501a1a-e20e-5de6-8dff-05d2a13f043a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244793} {:answer/id #uuid "483c0296-fd0f-53ac-b330-838f83260f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "483c0296-fd0f-53ac-b330-838f83260f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244794} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8496765-3856-5244-a44a-b1d2bf054d90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244789}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d91e312e-ed3c-5f08-9bb3-835259efe424" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-64681" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/94b8650466747f6858b30adbb8962e6c91159466b570f536d4999e2b08c31b67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4f797f8-5333-5654-8009-a4504ed3bcb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{21}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244797} {:answer/id #uuid "51cdc367-6c8b-51a1-8453-42bfb71c7ce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244798} {:answer/id #uuid "481c2906-cb72-5646-a6e8-7e71a18d38c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{21}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244799} {:answer/id #uuid "8846ac25-79b5-546c-a805-fec06d551902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244800} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5e91f9-3f04-500d-8571-ff14b7a7dae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8846ac25-79b5-546c-a805-fec06d551902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244800} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c6ecece-611e-55d9-9270-713ce24c643f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244796}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23da4612-2ac5-5804-a0b5-abd6a8ea6c5b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-65031" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{2}{5}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{4}{25} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c8aa141-74e5-52e9-acf2-64305e041c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244804} {:answer/id #uuid "0cccef28-4cf1-581a-a432-8c11ab47189e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244805} {:answer/id #uuid "89d444c2-70ee-59dc-9e8c-98526925de81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244806} {:answer/id #uuid "08e522fb-efa7-589d-8d5a-e302189ecfd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244807} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4eab83-bd33-5416-814c-fef0a5696ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08e522fb-efa7-589d-8d5a-e302189ecfd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244807} :answer-field/id #uuid "62102b6a-b890-5c79-8e99-8587f9b3e1ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244803}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efa71385-82f4-5742-a97f-9e1e4ed6258e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-65059" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/5d292a3598346bd05511c9c8979ba42845a88afe75a4740d8c4bdb9aa3968c87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/488ff32187c9a9a335e3782fb717242d899f622c43010662e28edcabe0e1bde3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{5}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{56}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f109b8-195d-57da-a72e-11244a6c22c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244811} {:answer/id #uuid "10b0291a-472e-5a71-bf30-084931436f2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244812} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2cb4be-53e8-5794-be39-15402508ff13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244813} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1e54a3-2591-57be-a6be-9e6929fdb0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244814} {:answer/id #uuid "028f5505-8e3d-5295-bdbd-78e879ac34bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10b0291a-472e-5a71-bf30-084931436f2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244812} :answer-field/id #uuid "804831b1-c89f-52c2-9a7b-85aaaabda928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244810}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e36e5e3-f31b-56d5-ac07-708bf215be4b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-65062" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/43629ebfaa2f9811135ffb15f5e5a59b796b83e3a525b4ab480efb9840d40879.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sec⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/f5219a363cdf18cfb628148ca7b9bbe6015b521799ed4487fa9b56843eaa3043.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{3}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{6}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5888265-a507-5860-9981-8afecb17c71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244818} {:answer/id #uuid "071315d9-a698-5cdb-9149-7474f93cc974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244819} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbfbd72-cb35-5d5b-bb60-1742fdfed611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244820} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec19daf-d813-5220-8c1e-f9cca3e1f725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244821} {:answer/id #uuid "13bc81d1-21a6-5e6b-8669-18095376079a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dbfbd72-cb35-5d5b-bb60-1742fdfed611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244820} :answer-field/id #uuid "416ca512-9b3e-51ce-9dd8-4cc34baee168" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244817}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9fcc86d-d9ab-5e33-bf4c-8062ae1074d7" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-65064" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/455df038e550d78fc29463c1a2e22dd44838d24af7428a824f9046fecc1ac1db.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $csc⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/44a555d222e3f6ff85208e8722a728ad240c558de7982d2a480bf9ddfb23ac6f.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{15}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ebf9e95-206d-5aa3-8e64-716ac134bca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244825} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa4a5d2-6cb9-5b19-85cb-8430fb21ba50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244826} {:answer/id #uuid "64a8d36d-4f29-54df-ad2e-2faf91badf75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244827} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd2c4fb-a25a-5040-aa50-9f7a883f5ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244828} {:answer/id #uuid "68e5edb0-70a4-5bbc-904d-c4589b903f8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cd2c4fb-a25a-5040-aa50-9f7a883f5ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244828} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fc8ad91-eb20-5237-9ef7-a32e820db7ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244824}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3e6cefb-e8cf-5ed7-bbb5-0526a87a8f04" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-65072" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/fc291f1fc5c4a712d3eef7ea2872de621855669d97445b24a2bc7a9da55949d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{4}{5})}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cdae874-74c2-5cdc-ade9-9d42f95435d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109710} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1d41af-b924-5aac-a84d-8c035820721d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109711} {:answer/id #uuid "defe7f3d-b6f8-5990-8c98-819c312f60cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{45}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109712} {:answer/id #uuid "07648329-6039-5609-860e-c78881ef81fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109713} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4e9d83-f971-55cc-b350-a5387323290a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad1d41af-b924-5aac-a84d-8c035820721d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109711} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a2a25d-67a7-5b6d-8a3a-8e7117aac155" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109709}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0efeeff3-47fe-5165-905e-7ef041b1d387" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-65073" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1453/Source/Images/q-65073.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\tan θ$." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/21170c11aa5ff0ab39b86f94588e5e8a0fa178d0c34b9aa1774ee9f07a4d0812.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{45}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}{(\\frac{2}{7})}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0ceb33-bd4e-56f6-b55b-e2fe36cdbaef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244832} {:answer/id #uuid "f81e5f8a-85fb-5bd0-8432-9704dec15e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244833} {:answer/id #uuid "4dae3bae-9b83-5864-98d2-c2db6f7e1047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244834} {:answer/id #uuid "da368431-1e1c-5e9a-8ff4-2ff4a9d70651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{17}}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244835} {:answer/id #uuid "e143bc21-e4b9-5775-be89-e4bdc36f2781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dae3bae-9b83-5864-98d2-c2db6f7e1047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244834} :answer-field/id #uuid "da0ab566-1f3f-5e9f-badc-d7e6ac8b96aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244831}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73ad884a-c368-5c61-8236-2c71db37c8cc" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-65075" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/d846227996dd5fc1d6c3ca30108f548ac0d3743ec9262db0a551d28a32386996.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $cot⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/5284ae5a30a5d370d52c887cc6222ee4991a98d087dc751cbcfe5d7307ca1d47.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{17}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{9}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{cos⁡\\theta }{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9})}{(\\frac{8}{9})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77e32851-2728-5f0e-850f-e5e552e3fd54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244839} {:answer/id #uuid "a36a031e-b8d1-52c2-9950-a487589efc75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244840} {:answer/id #uuid "72c8bec3-9779-5965-b7c3-624b6cc495a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244841} {:answer/id #uuid "31967ba3-387f-53ce-a135-e33bb557b66e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244842} {:answer/id #uuid "787df602-c645-57d8-b9bc-d333db9962f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72c8bec3-9779-5965-b7c3-624b6cc495a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244841} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bac85b2-52aa-56cf-b6cb-30eb4b783a26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244838}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2cc7414-4ea5-59a5-aaa2-553fdb109e30" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-85234" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/87635426224ded01c8bf486c7f59eeb6cacbe4e5c7db964643910bb5d142f0f3.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sec⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/9f64bae56998bca7d825780c8c2b437ae3106486053cc9c8ed1b1fca2a88d4ab.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
